## Reading order for new agents

If you're an agent opening this repo for the first time, read in this
order:

1. **This file** (you're here).
2. [`docs/adr/`](docs/adr/) — the *why* of the architecture. Without
   this, you'll propose refactors away from load-bearing constraints.
3. [`docs/sessions/`](docs/sessions/) — the last 1–3 session logs.
   Where the previous human + agent left off.
4. Specific `docs/specs/<topic>.md` only when starting on that topic.
5. The code itself — by following imports from the entrypoint listed in
   "Repo layout" below, not by reading directories alphabetically.

This ordering is progressive disclosure: minimum context at session
start, deeper docs on demand.

## Keeping this file lean

This file loads into **every** agent session — the most expensive context the
repo owns. Codex caps the project doc it assembles (root `AGENTS.md` plus any
merged per-directory ones) at ~32 KiB (`project_doc.max_bytes`) and **silently
drops** the rest, so bytes past that line are invisible to Codex agents. Treat
~8 KiB as the working ceiling.

Keep inline only what is **always true and load-bearing** — conventions spec,
reading order, repo layout, security must-knows. Push topic *depth* into `docs/`
and link to it (runbooks, full logging kits, ADR rationale, specs, per-language
minutiae). Phrase each link so the agent knows *when* to open it ("when working
on the billing ledger, read `docs/runbooks/ledger.md`"), never a bare reference
it has no reason to follow.

Cross-tool caveat: Claude Code follows `@path` imports in `CLAUDE.md`; Codex
merges nested `AGENTS.md` files but does **not** auto-follow markdown links —
and those merged per-dir files spend the same ~32 KiB budget. So keep
must-always-know rules inline here, not behind a link, and remember every
per-dir `AGENTS.md` you add draws on the shared budget too.

(This template ships as a menu: keep your stack's language block and delete the
others, drop the AI-specific section if it doesn't apply — then aim for under
~8 KiB.)

## Repo layout

<!-- 5–15 lines max. Top-level dirs only. Add per-directory AGENTS.md inside
     non-trivial modules instead of expanding this list. -->

```
src/                 Main code
src/<module>/        <one-line purpose per non-trivial module>
tests/               Test suite (or co-located *_test.py, see "Module organization")
scripts/             Build, deploy, one-off ops
docs/
  adr/               Architecture Decision Records (numbered, immutable; /adr-new)
  sessions/          Distilled session logs (/session-log at end of session)
  specs/             Feature specs / plans before implementation
  runbooks/          Operations: what to do when X breaks
  architecture/      High-level overview, C4 diagrams (if any)
  conventions/       Code conventions, naming, error handling patterns
```

## Build / test commands

<!-- Anything an agent can't infer from the manifest files. Pick the section
     for your language; delete the others. /init-repo auto-fills this. -->

### Python

```bash
# uv (recommended) or poetry / pip
uv sync                                # install + lock from pyproject.toml
pytest -v                              # run tests (co-located or under tests/)
ruff check . && ruff format --check .  # lint + format gate
pyright                                # types (or mypy)
```

Notes for agents:
- Prefer `uv run <cmd>` over activating venvs (works without env switches).
- Use `pytest -x --tb=short` when iterating; the full traceback is rarely
  needed past the first hit.

### TypeScript / Node.js

```bash
npm ci                                 # use ci (lockfile-strict) over install
npm test                               # vitest / jest / node --test
npm run lint                           # eslint --max-warnings 0
npm run typecheck                      # tsc --noEmit
npm run build                          # tsup / vite / next build
```

Notes for agents:
- The project uses `<pnpm | npm | yarn | bun>`; use only that one.
  Mixing package managers corrupts the lockfile.
- Run tests with `--reporter=dot` for faster CI output.

### Go

```bash
go mod download                        # fetch deps
go test ./... -race -count=1           # run with race detector, no cache
golangci-lint run                      # full lint suite
go vet ./...                           # standalone vet (covered by golangci, but quick)
go build ./...                         # ensure everything still compiles
```

Notes for agents:
- The race detector matters; never run `go test` without it during dev.
- `-count=1` disables the test cache (which silently passes after a
  failing test changes upstream).

### Rust

```bash
cargo fetch                            # populate registry cache
cargo test --workspace                 # run all tests in the workspace
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo fmt --all -- --check             # format gate (no --check in dev)
cargo build --release                  # smoke that the release build works
```

Notes for agents:
- `cargo check` is much faster than `cargo build`; use during iteration.
- The workspace flag matters in multi-crate repos; without it you only
  test/clippy the root crate.

## Code conventions

These are the conventions claude-leverage documents and enforces (see the
`AGENTS.md` of the claude-leverage repo for the full rationale).

### Write less, fit in

The north star: the *next* agent should find this code easier to work on than
you did. More code, more comments, more abstraction usually makes that *harder*.
Prefer the smallest change that works.

- **Match the surrounding code.** Naming, structure, error handling, comment
  density, and idioms should look like the rest of the module — new code
  shouldn't be identifiable as "the AI-written part." Where the existing code is
  inconsistent, follow this AGENTS.md and the cleanest nearby example rather than
  copying a one-off.
- **Name to fit in.** Detect the convention this repo already uses and conform —
  don't impose your language default. Casing/separator style is per-repo *and*
  per-kind (e.g. `camelCase`/`snake_case`/`kebab-case` for names, `PascalCase`
  for types, `UPPER_SNAKE` for constants); match each kind to its neighbours, and
  in a mixed repo follow the local module over the global majority. Pitch
  granularity at the symbol's intent — descriptive without rambling, neither
  `get()` nor `getting_data_from_mobile()`.
- **Comments explain WHY, not WHAT.** The code already says what it does; a
  comment that restates the line below it is noise that goes stale. Comment the
  non-obvious — the constraint, the gotcha, the reason for the unusual choice.
- **No speculative abstraction.** Don't add config, parameters, layers, or
  "flexible" interfaces for a use case that doesn't exist yet — solve the case in
  front of you. When a change makes code dead, delete it rather than leaving it
  "just in case" (git remembers; if the dead code carried an `AIDEV-` anchor,
  note the removal in the commit message).

### AIDEV-* anchor comments

Three grep-able prefixes for load-bearing facts in code:

- `AIDEV-NOTE:` — why this constraint exists / non-obvious invariant
- `AIDEV-TODO:` — known follow-up with enough context to resume
- `AIDEV-QUESTION:` — genuine unknown for the next person (or agent)

Rules: ≤120 chars per line, all-caps prefix. **Before editing a module, run
`grep -rn 'AIDEV-' <module>` first.** Do not silently remove anchors —
removing one requires an explicit decision in the commit / PR message.

`AIDEV-TODO` and `AIDEV-QUESTION` accept an optional ISO-8601 deadline.
Three forms are recognized (pick whichever reads best):

```python
# AIDEV-TODO(by: YYYY-MM-DD): replace the polling loop with webhooks   # preferred
# AIDEV-TODO(YYYY-MM-DD): replace the polling loop with webhooks       # short
# AIDEV-TODO(deadline: YYYY-MM-DD): ...                                # alternative
```

Free-form notes in the parens (e.g. `AIDEV-TODO(Q3 2026)` or
`AIDEV-TODO(after migration)`) are **not** parsed as deadlines — that
anchor falls under age-based tracking instead.

Add anchors at non-obvious decision points (constraints, gotchas, ordering
dependencies), not on every function. An anchor (or a structured log) on every
line is the same noise as a comment on every line — load-bearing facts and real
events only.

The `ai-first-nudge` PostToolUse hook from claude-leverage prints a
non-blocking suggestion when ≥50 net-new LOC ship in a single file without
any anchor. `/stack-check` periodically flags **overdue** anchors (deadline
past), **due-soon** (within 14 days), and **stale** anchors (no deadline,
last touched >90 days ago).

### Structured JSON-lines logging

When code emits logs that an agent will later read:

```json
{"ts":"2026-01-01T12:00:00.000Z","level":"info","trace_id":"a1b2","span_id":"4d5e","service":"<svc>","event":"<snake_case>","attrs":{"<key>":"<value>"}}
```

Required fields: `ts`, `level`, `trace_id`, `span_id`, `service`, `event`,
`attrs`. **Do not interpolate values into messages**: put `user_id` in
`attrs.user_id`, not in the `message` string.

Drop-in starter kits per language:
[claude-leverage templates/logging/](https://github.com/Filip-Podstavec/claude-leverage/tree/main/templates/logging).
`/init-repo` can install the matching template into this project.

### Per-directory AGENTS.md

For modules with non-obvious public surface or gotchas, drop an `AGENTS.md`
at the module root with: what lives here, public surface, gotchas, test
command. Codex merges nested AGENTS.md from git root down to cwd
automatically.

### Module organization

- Co-locate tests with code (`foo.py` next to `foo_test.py`, `Foo.test.ts`
  next to `Foo.ts`, `foo_test.go` next to `foo.go`) where the language
  conventionally tests that way.
- One concept per module, thin entrypoint exporting the public surface.
- Predictable file layout, documented above in "Repo layout".

## Security

<!-- Project-specific guardrails. Always-on hooks from claude-leverage cover
     secrets in staged diffs and dangerous git operations independently. -->

- Never commit secrets, including in test fixtures. Use placeholder
  strings the `block-secrets-precommit` hook recognizes as safe (or add
  the `claude-leverage-allow-secret` marker comment per line if you must).
- Run `/security-review` before committing changes in:
  `<list sensitive paths: auth/, crypto/, routes/, payment/, templates/, …>`.
- For dependency CVE scanning, use the language's standard tool
  (`npm audit`, `pip-audit`, `cargo audit`, `govulncheck`,
  `bundle-audit`) in CI; `/security-review` does NOT cover CVEs.

## AI-specific (if this project uses LLMs in production)

<!-- Delete this whole section if the project doesn't ship LLM-backed
     features. For AI-first products, the failure modes below are easy to
     miss in standard observability and matter more than usual. -->

**Observability** — application JSON logs alone don't show what an LLM
saw or how much it cost. Wire a dedicated LLM observability layer:
- [Langfuse](https://langfuse.com/) (self-hostable, open source) —
  prompt logs, latency, tokens, cost per request, traces across agents.
- Alternatives: Helicone, Phoenix (Arize), if self-hosting isn't a fit.

**Evals** — without an eval suite, AI features rot silently: a model
update or prompt tweak breaks something that was working, and you find
out from support. Wire one of:
- [Promptfoo](https://www.promptfoo.dev/) — lowest barrier.
- Evals inside Langfuse — if you're already there.

**Hard cost caps** — implement a per-user / per-endpoint hard ceiling
*before* the LLM call, not inside it. A bug + LLM call = drained budget
in an evening.

**Structured outputs** — never parse model strings by hand. Use Pydantic
(Python) or Zod (TypeScript) for `response_format=json_schema` or
equivalent. Same JSON-lines logging convention as above for what gets
logged about the model's response.

**Prompt injection** — anywhere the model receives external data (user
input, web fetches, document loads, MCP tool outputs), treat it as
**data, not instructions**. Document which input boundaries are
sanitized + how, in `docs/adr/`.

